# Evaluate a Function

In this notebook you measure a plain Elixir function against labeled cases.
You write a shipping-price function, give Tune three test rows, add two
scores, and read the per-case results and the score summaries. The notebook
makes no model calls and needs no credentials.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tune, "~> 0.5.0"}
])
```

## Write the function under test

The function prices a parcel from its weight and delivery zone. It is ordinary
Elixir code. Tune does not need to know anything about it.

```elixir
defmodule Shipping do
  @rates %{"local" => 5.0, "national" => 9.0}

  def quote(%{weight_kg: weight, zone: zone}) do
    %{price: Map.get(@rates, zone, 12.0) + weight * 1.5}
  end
end

Shipping.quote(%{weight_kg: 1, zone: "local"})
```

```text
%{price: 6.5}
```

## Describe the test cases

Each row has an `id`, an `input`, and an optional `expected` value. Tune passes
only `input` to the function. Scores see the whole case, so labels never reach
the code under test.

The third row has no `expected` value. You use it to see how Tune reports a
score that fails on one case.

```elixir
rows = [
  %{id: "small-local", input: %{weight_kg: 1, zone: "local"}, expected: %{price: 6.5}},
  %{id: "heavy-national", input: %{weight_kg: 10, zone: "national"}, expected: %{price: 24.0}},
  %{id: "unlabeled", input: %{weight_kg: 2, zone: "local"}}
]

length(rows)
```

```text
3
```

## Build the pipeline

`Tune.new/2` takes the target and a runner. The runner receives the target and
one case input, and returns the output. Here the target is the `Shipping`
module, and the runner calls `quote/1`. In your own code, pass the function you
already use in production.

`Tune.data/2` attaches the rows as the `:test` role. Tune reads them only when
the pipeline runs.

Add two independent scores with `Tune.score/4`:

- `:exact` is a two-argument scorer. It reads the case and the output, and
  compares the price with the label.
- `:under_20` is a one-argument scorer. It reads only the output.

With more than one score, `Tune.select/2` names the score that decides the
headline value.

```elixir
pipeline =
  Shipping
  |> Tune.new(run: fn shipping, input -> shipping.quote(input) end)
  |> Tune.data(test: rows)
  |> Tune.score(:exact, fn tune_case, output ->
    if output.price == tune_case.expected.price, do: 1, else: 0
  end)
  |> Tune.score(:under_20, fn output -> if output.price < 20, do: 1, else: 0 end)
  |> Tune.select(:under_20)

pipeline.scores |> Enum.map(& &1.name)
```

```text
[:exact, :under_20]
```

Building the pipeline runs nothing. Each builder call returns a new `%Tune{}`
value.

## Run the evaluation

`Tune.evaluate/2` runs the function on every `:test` row and scores each
output. It returns `{:ok, %Tune.Evaluation{}}`.

```elixir
{:ok, evaluation} = Tune.evaluate(pipeline)

{evaluation.complete?, evaluation.eligible?, evaluation.stop_reason}
```

```text
{true, true, :completed}
```

## Inspect each case

`evaluation.observations` keeps one `Tune.Observation` per case, in row order.
Each observation holds the case `id`, its `status`, the `output`, the numeric
`scores`, and any `score_errors`.

```elixir
for observation <- evaluation.observations do
  IO.puts(
    "#{observation.id}: #{observation.status} " <>
      "price=#{observation.output.price} " <>
      "scores=#{inspect(observation.scores)} " <>
      "score_errors=#{inspect(Map.keys(observation.score_errors))}"
  )
end

:ok
```

```text
small-local: ok price=6.5 scores=%{exact: 1, under_20: 1} score_errors=[]
heavy-national: ok price=24.0 scores=%{exact: 1, under_20: 0} score_errors=[]
unlabeled: error price=8.0 scores=%{under_20: 1} score_errors=[:exact]
```

The function produced an output for `unlabeled`, but the `:exact` scorer
raised because the row has no `expected` map. Tune records that as a score
error on the case and marks the observation `:error`. The `:under_20` score for
the same case still counts.

Read the error to see what went wrong:

```elixir
unlabeled = List.last(evaluation.observations)
Exception.message(unlabeled.score_errors.exact)
```

```text
"the score raised: expected a map, got:\n\n    nil\n"
```

The error is a `Tune.Error.Execution.Callback`. A score that raises, exits, or
returns something other than a number or `%{score: number, feedback: text}`
produces this error. The evaluation itself still returns `{:ok, evaluation}`.

## Read the score summaries

`evaluation.summaries` holds one `Tune.Score.Summary` per score.
`Tune.Evaluation.summary/2` returns one by name. `count` is the number of
scored cases, and `coverage` is the share of cases that produced that score.

```elixir
for name <- [:exact, :under_20] do
  summary = Tune.Evaluation.summary(evaluation, name)
  IO.puts("#{name}: mean=#{summary.mean} count=#{summary.count} coverage=#{Float.round(summary.coverage, 2)}")
end

evaluation.selected_score
```

```text
exact: mean=1.0 count=2 coverage=0.67
under_20: mean=0.6666666666666666 count=3 coverage=1.0
0.6666666666666666
```

The `:exact` mean is 1.0, but it covers only two of three cases. Always read a
mean together with its coverage. `selected_score` is the mean of the selected
score, `:under_20`.

The evaluation stays eligible because the selected score covers every case. If
`:exact` were the selected score, the missing case would make the evaluation
ineligible and `selected_score` would be `nil`. Pass `failure_score:` to
`Tune.score/4` when a failed case should count as a fixed value instead.

